# Performance Report: Concurrent Shortest Path Finder

All numbers below were **measured**, not estimated. Reproduce them with:

```bash
python benchmark.py --repeats 3
```

`benchmark.py` seeds the random generator (`--seed 42` by default), builds each graph with
`generate_random_graph` (weights uniform in [1, 10]; the "negative" case flips ~10% of edges
negative), and reports the best of 3 timings (`time.perf_counter`) for each solver on the same graph.

## Test setup (this run)

- Machine: 2-vCPU Linux (aarch64) cloud sandbox
- Python 3.12.15 (CPython, with the GIL)
- Date: 2026-10-30

## Results

| Graph | Nodes | Edges | Threads | Negative edges | Sequential (s) | Concurrent (s) | Speedup | Result |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Small | 100 | 458 | 1 | no | 0.0002 | 0.0013 | 0.15x | 0 mismatches |
| Small | 100 | 458 | 8 | no | 0.0002 | 0.0022 | 0.08x | 0 mismatches |
| Medium | 1000 | 9951 | 1 | no | 0.0054 | 0.0226 | 0.24x | 0 mismatches |
| Medium | 1000 | 9951 | 8 | no | 0.0056 | 0.0298 | 0.19x | 0 mismatches |
| Large sparse | 5000 | 24602 | 8 | no | 0.0407 | 0.1999 | 0.20x | 0 mismatches |
| Medium, ~10% negative edges | 1000 | 9940 | 8 | yes | 1.1621 | 4.3288 | 0.27x | seq: negative cycle, conc: negative cycle |

Speedup = sequential time / concurrent time. A value below 1.00x means the concurrent solver was **slower**.

An earlier captured run on a Windows machine (`output_utf8.md`, 2,000 nodes, edge_prob 0.01,
~10% negative edges, 8 threads) shows the same pattern: sequential 5.177 s vs concurrent 16.488 s,
both correctly reporting a negative-weight cycle.

## Analysis

- **The thread-pool version does not beat the sequential version in CPython.** Edge relaxation is
  pure-Python, CPU-bound work, so the Global Interpreter Lock lets only one thread run Python
  bytecode at a time. Threads add task-submission overhead and a `threading.Lock` acquire/release
  for every edge relaxed, so the concurrent solver ran roughly 4-13x slower in every case measured.
- **Correctness holds.** Both solvers produced identical distances on every graph without a
  negative cycle (0 mismatches), and both detected the negative-weight cycle in the negative-edge case.
- **Early exit dominates on positive-weight graphs.** With only positive weights the distances
  settle in a few rounds, so both solvers finish quickly. With a negative cycle, relaxation never
  settles and both solvers run all V-1 rounds before the detection pass, which is why that case is
  orders of magnitude slower.
- More threads did not help: 8 threads were no faster than 1 on the same graph.

## Conclusion

The concurrent implementation is a correct, thread-safe Bellman-Ford, but under CPython it is
slower than the sequential baseline. Real speedups would need true parallelism, for example
`multiprocessing` with shared memory, a free-threaded (no-GIL) Python build, or moving the
relaxation loop into NumPy/C/Rust, together with per-thread update buffers instead of a lock per edge.
